    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d755">

                <pb facs="1148" n="1134" xml:id="pg1134"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">501.BB Korea/2–2748: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Acting Political Adviser
                        in Korea</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Langdon</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">us urgent</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-02-27">February 27,
                        1948</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>59. In event Gen. Hodge feels it would serve useful purpose in Korea, Dept would
                    have no objection his issuing statement along following lines: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“My Government welcomes the action of the Interim Commission of the
                            United Nations in adopting its resolution of February 26 expressing its
                            view that the United Nations Temporary Commission on Korea should
                            proceed with its task as stated in the resolution of the General
                            Assembly adopted on November 14, 1947 in such parts of Korea as are
                            accessible to it. My Government is happy to renew its pledge of the full
                            support and cooperation of the United States authorities both in Korea
                            and in Washington for the successful conclusion of those tasks. This
                            development is in keeping with the declared objectives of the United
                            States with respect to Korea and the Korean people: (1) To establish a
                            self-governing, sovereign Korea as soon as possible, independent of
                            foreign control and eligible for membership in the United Nations; (2)
                            to insure that the national government so established shall be fully
                            representative of the freely expressed will of the Korean people; and
                            (3) to assist the Koreans in establishing a sound economy and adequate
                            educational system as essential bases of an independent, democratic
                                state”.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d755-f1"/>
                        </p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Marshall</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d755-f1" n="1" xml:id="d755-f1-content">
                <p>Telegram 123, March 2, from Seoul, reported that General Hodge had issued the
                    statement on February 28, but had changed point (3) to read: “To assist the
                    Koreans in establishing a sound economy essential for an independent, democratic
                    state.” (501.BB Korea/3–248)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
